  "result": "**Root cause:** `parseAmount` in `src/money.js` used a regex that only accepted plain digits. A price with a thousands separator, such as `\"2,450.50\"`, didn't match, so `match()` returned `null`. The next line then read `m[1]` and threw `TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading '1')`, which surfaced as a 500 on `POST /checkout`.\n\nThe log has 37 such errors, and the debug line before each one shows a price like `\"2,450.50\"` or `\"1,299.00\"`. None of the 37 had any other price shape. Orders with prices under 1,000 succeeded, which is why it looked intermittent.\n\n**Fix (`src/money.js`):**\n- `parseAmount` now accepts comma-grouped whole parts (`1,234,567.00`) and strips the commas before parsing.\n- Input that still doesn't match throws the documented `Error('Invalid amount: …')` instead of a `TypeError`.\n\n**Tests (`test/money.test.js`):** I added three, covering:\n- grouped amounts through `parseAmount`\n- malformed input (`abc`, empty string, `12,34`, `1,2345.00`, `-5`) throwing the clear error\n- an end-to-end `checkout` with a `\"2,450.50\"` price\n\n`npm test` passes with 7 of 7. I did not run the new tests against the old code to confirm they fail there.\n\nI read the failures only from the log lines. How `server.js` turns a thrown error into a response is untouched. With this fix, a genuinely invalid price still throws, so you may want to return a 400 for it rather than a 500.",
